// Bit standard library — sync module, Mutex.
//
// Channels remain the preferred concurrency primitive (CSP, SPEC §16); this
// module is for shared-memory hot paths and coordinating parallel workers
// under M:N scheduling, where routing every access through a channel is more
// overhead than the data warrants.
//
// Mutex is built on a capacity-1 channel holding a single token. Receiving on
// an empty channel already parks the green thread via the scheduler rather
// than busy-spinning (SPEC §16.2, proven under M:N by the runtime's channel
// parking path) — reusing that mechanism gives lock() its required parking
// behavior for free, with no new runtime primitive.

// `Mutex()` is a Mutex ready to use, unlocked. A class value built any other
// way (the zero value has an uninitialized `chan` field) would block forever on
// a nil channel in lock()/unlock(), which is why the class owns an `init`.
export class Mutex {
  token: chan<bool>
  init() {
    this.token = chan<bool>(1)
    this.token <- true
  }
  // Blocks until the lock is free, then acquires it. Contention parks the
  // caller on the scheduler; it does not spin.
  export lock() {
    <- this.token
  }

  // Releases the lock. Unlocking a Mutex not held by the caller is a caller
  // bug — like Go's sync.Mutex, this does not detect misuse.
  export unlock() {
    this.token <- true
  }
}

